You might not have to squish anything; the inlays can be shallow and
they often are extensive. Besides, if "silver spellbook" really refers
to actual silver in the cover, I'd expect it to refer to locks, chains
and other mountings more than anything. Some particularly rich covers
were all metal, inlaid with gems. But those were _very_ expensive; not
something you leave lying in a cave.
I presume, therefore, that a "silver spellbook" is only silver in
appearance, much like an "emerald potion" isn't really made of gemstone.
Or possibly that, like a "dog-eared spellbook", it refers to the pages,
not to the cover. Even a normally expensive book could have silver leaf
decorations on the important pages, but have a simple tooled leather
cover.

